## Both C library variants are carried

They are not one source built two ways. glibc ships three modules uclibc does
not:

    mi_jpd.ko  mi_vdec.ko  mi_vdisp.ko

    uclibc  9.1.0   19 modules
    glibc  11.1.0   22 modules

and mi_sys.ko differs between them. This mirrors the userspace split in
sigmastar-lib, where glibc likewise carries two libraries uclibc lacks. The C
library component of the path selects content, not merely codegen, so a build
that picks the wrong one is missing modules rather than getting differently
compiled ones.

`soc/sigmastar/infinity6c.mk` will have to name which variant a port uses, via
SIGMASTAR_LIBC and SIGMASTAR_GCC, and it must be the same choice sigmastar-lib
is indexed with.

## MI modules only

The vendor's `modules/5.10` holds 143 (uclibc) / 146 (glibc) modules, of which
120-odd are prebuilt sensor drivers. Those are not carried: sensors are built
from `sensor-src/` here as they are for every other family, which is what keeps
them independent of the vendor's kernel build.

## Stripped of debug info

The vendor ships these unstripped -- 14 `.debug*` sections each, 59M (uclibc)
and 37M (glibc) for the MI modules alone. They are carried stripped:

    arm-thingino-linux-gnueabihf-strip --strip-debug mi_*.ko

    uclibc  59M -> 3.7M
    glibc   37M -> 4.0M

Checked rather than assumed, on mi_sys.ko: the exported symbol set is identical
(176 `__ksymtab_*` entries before and after), the imported symbol count is
unchanged at 236, and `vermagic`, `depends=mi_common` and the Sigmastar build
stamp all survive. Only `.debug*` sections are gone. The infinity6e modules
beside this carry no debug sections either, having been through a firmware
image, so this also makes the two families consistent.

This is the one place the repository modifies vendor bytes, so the md5s below
are of the stripped files. The originals are recoverable by re-running the strip
above against the vendor path named earlier:

    mi_sys.ko  uclibc   vendor c9e29a1f...  here 2ff20927...
    mi_sys.ko  glibc    vendor 91515d8c...  here 6de03cec...

## sensor-src/

10 drivers: 8 carried here, plus imx335 and imx415 symlinked across to
infinity6e, which is how upstream ships them. Source, compiled at build time.
Origin and the reason for the per-family split are in the top-level PROVENANCE.

These were removed in 4b08ea4 as an unreachable family and restored here from
cca10f4 when the family gained payload.

## iqfile/

`isp_api.xml` is the vendor's own, unlike infinity6e's, which matches no vendor
copy.

`iqfile0.bin` is the vendor's default CUS3A iqfile. The vendor ships it under
two sensor names -- `iqfile/sc430ai/sc430ai_iqfile.bin` and
`iqfile/imx307/imx307_iqfile.bin` -- and the two are **byte-identical**, so it
is one file here under the name CUS3A reads. Note this is unlike infinity6e,
where `iqfile0.bin` really is one sensor's tuning (imx415's) standing in as a
default.

## sensor-iq/

Per-sensor API bins, from the vendor's `iqfile/<sensor>/<SENSOR>_MIPI_api.bin`,
renamed to the lowercase driver name that raptor resolves tuning by. The vendor
ships these for two sensors only, both genuinely distinct:

    sc430ai.bin   56737fd6
    imx307.bin    c6bf43fd

An HDR variant, `SC430AI_MIPI_HDR_api.bin`, exists in the vendor tree and is not
carried -- nothing selects it, and the runtime resolves by driver name alone.

## venc_fw/

`chagall.bin` is VENC firmware. The vendor's own i6c copy, and unlike
infinity6e's it does match the vendor tree. Per chip: the i6c copies differ from
i6e's and from each other across releases.

## The kernel these pair with is not settled

johnchia/linux `sigmastar-infinity6c` is **not** built from this drop, so loading
these modules on it is unverified.

Its BSP import, b335d21 "0000-infinity6c-kernel-5.10.61.patch", is authored by
OpenIPC's Viktor in November 2023 and sits directly on vanilla Linux 5.10.61
(230ca533). Compared against this SDK's own kernel source:

    drivers/sstar          118 of ~1300 files differ
    whole tree             255 files differ, 60 exist only in the branch
                           (infinity6c DTS and defconfigs the SDK lacks)

Of the three SSC377 drops, release_0907 is the closest -- 1183 identical BSP
files against 1157 for 0712 and 1091 for 0602, and only 21 files it lacks
against 60 and 71 -- which independently corroborates the choice of drop. It is
still not a match, and the patch postdating all three by a year suggests it was
cut from a newer SigmaStar release.

**vermagic will not catch this.** b335d21's own i6c defconfig sets !SMP,
PREEMPT, MODULE_UNLOAD and THUMB2_KERNEL, which composes exactly the string
these modules carry:

    5.10.61 preempt mod_unload ARMv7 thumb2 p2v8

so insmod would accept them, and with CONFIG_MODVERSIONS off any ABI drift among
those 118 files appears later as symbol resolution failure or misbehaviour. The
differing files include cam_os_wrapper.c/.h and cam_os_export.c, which carry the
CamOs* symbols the sensor drivers bind to.

Before a port ships, either move the kernel branch to the BSP these were built
against, or replace these with modules from the drop b335d21 was cut from.
Whichever, record it here.

## Not consumed yet

No thingino target builds Infinity6C -- there is no `soc/sigmastar/infinity6c.mk`
and so no SOC_FAMILY that resolves to this directory. This is staged for a port,
alongside the userspace half in sigmastar-lib, which the Raptor i6c backend
needs. Nothing here has been loaded on hardware.

No source is available for the prebuilt artifacts and none is claimed here.

